---
import GuideLayout from "@/layouts/GuideLayout.astro";
import { GUIDES_BY_SLUG } from "@/data/guides";
const g = GUIDES_BY_SLUG["salary-vs-hourly-pay"]!;
---

<GuideLayout title={g.title} metaTitle={g.metaTitle} description={g.description} slug={g.slug} updated={g.updated}>
  <p>
    A salaried worker earns a fixed amount each pay period regardless of exact
    hours. An hourly worker is paid for each hour recorded. That one structural
    difference ripples through your overtime rights, income stability, benefits,
    and how you should compare two job offers.
  </p>

  <h2>The core difference</h2>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>&nbsp;</th><th>Salary</th><th>Hourly</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Paycheck</td><td>Fixed each period</td><td>Varies with hours worked</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Overtime</td><td>Only if non-exempt</td><td>Yes (non-exempt), usually 1.5&times; over 40 hrs/week</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Short weeks</td><td>Usually paid in full</td><td>Paid only for hours worked</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Extra hours</td><td>Often unpaid (if exempt)</td><td>Paid, often at a premium</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Benefits</td><td>More often included</td><td>Varies; sometimes limited or pro-rated</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Schedule</td><td>More flexible, more "always on"</td><td>More defined start/stop</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Exempt vs non-exempt decides overtime</h2>
  <p>
    Being paid a salary does <em>not</em> by itself remove your right to
    overtime. Under the US Fair Labor Standards Act you are exempt only if you
    meet a salary-basis test, a salary-level test, and a duties test. Everyone
    else — including many salaried employees — is non-exempt and must receive
    overtime for hours over 40 in a workweek. The full three-part test is in
    <a href="/guides/exempt-vs-non-exempt-employees/">exempt vs non-exempt
    employees</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Pros and cons of salary</h2>
  <p><strong>Upsides:</strong></p>
  <ul>
    <li>A predictable paycheck, even in weeks with a holiday or a slow patch.</li>
    <li>Usually stronger benefits: paid leave, retirement match, health coverage.</li>
    <li>More schedule autonomy in many roles.</li>
    <li>Often a signal of seniority, which can help future negotiation.</li>
  </ul>
  <p><strong>Downsides:</strong></p>
  <ul>
    <li>If you are exempt, extra hours are unpaid — a 55-hour week pays the same as a 40-hour week.</li>
    <li>Your <em>effective</em> hourly rate falls as your hours rise.</li>
    <li>Blurred boundaries between work and personal time.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Pros and cons of hourly</h2>
  <p><strong>Upsides:</strong></p>
  <ul>
    <li>Every hour is paid, and overtime is paid at a premium.</li>
    <li>Clear boundaries — when the shift ends, work ends.</li>
    <li>Picking up extra shifts directly increases pay.</li>
  </ul>
  <p><strong>Downsides:</strong></p>
  <ul>
    <li>Income drops in short weeks, holidays, or when hours are cut.</li>
    <li>Benefits may be thinner or require a minimum-hours threshold.</li>
    <li>Less schedule control; last-minute changes are more common.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The "promotion to salary" trap</h2>
  <p>
    Moving from an hourly role with regular overtime to a salaried exempt role
    can be a pay cut in disguise. If you earned $25/hour and routinely worked
    48-hour weeks, your gross was roughly <code>(40 &times; 25 + 8 &times;
    37.50) &times; 52 = $67,600</code>. A "$65,000 salary" promotion with no
    overtime is less money for the same hours. Always compare against your
    <em>actual</em> recent earnings, not your base rate.
  </p>

  <h2>Comparing two offers on equal terms</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Convert the salary to an hourly figure: <code>salary &divide; (hours per week &times; weeks per year)</code>, usually &divide; 2,080. See <a href="/guides/convert-hourly-wage-to-annual-salary/">converting an hourly wage to a salary</a>.</li>
    <li>Adjust for the hours each job really expects. A role that wants 50-hour weeks has a much lower real rate than salary &divide; 2,080 suggests.</li>
    <li>Add expected overtime to the hourly offer; exempt salaried roles pay none.</li>
    <li>Value the benefits separately — paid leave, employer health contribution, and retirement match can be worth several thousand dollars a year.</li>
    <li>Compare <em>take-home</em>, not gross, if the pre-tax benefit packages differ — see <a href="/guides/gross-pay-vs-take-home-pay/">gross pay vs take-home pay</a>.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    The <a href="/calculators/salary-to-hourly-calculator/">Salary to Hourly
    Calculator</a> does the conversion, shows the effective rate after paid time
    off, and compares up to three amounts side by side.
  </p>

  <h2>A third option: contract and gig work</h2>
  <p>
    Independent contractors set their own rate but receive no overtime, no paid
    leave, and no employer benefits, and they pay both halves of Social Security
    and Medicare (<a href="/guides/what-are-fica-taxes/">SECA</a>). A contract
    rate usually needs to be 25&ndash;50% above an equivalent salary to break
    even. Misclassifying an employee as a contractor to avoid these costs is
    unlawful.
  </p>

  <h2>Which is better?</h2>
  <p>
    Neither, universally. Salary suits predictable roles and people who value a
    steady paycheck and benefits. Hourly suits variable schedules, roles with
    regular overtime, and anyone who wants to be paid for every hour. Read the
    offer for expected hours, the overtime policy, benefits eligibility, and
    whether the role is classified exempt or non-exempt.
  </p>

  <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
  <h3>Is salary always better than hourly?</h3>
  <p>
    No. If you work significant overtime, a non-exempt hourly role can pay more
    for the same hours because of the 1.5&times; premium.
  </p>
  <h3>Can a salaried employee get overtime?</h3>
  <p>
    Yes, if they are non-exempt. The salary is then divided by the hours it
    covers to find the regular rate, and overtime is paid on top.
  </p>

